; `.default` at a MANDATORY member position, and the same control carried in through an ALIAS rule.
; RFC 8610 §3.8.2 makes a default meaningful only for an OPTIONAL occurrence — it substitutes for an
; ABSENT value, and a mandatory member is never absent — so the generator strips it (warning on
; stderr) at field construction. `b` and `x` below therefore emit as PLAIN mandatory fields that keep
; their `new()` arguments, which is what keeps the wasm and WIT constructors (both mirror `new()`) in
; agreement with the rust one.
;
; The alias rule is why the decision is per USE SITE rather than a refusal: `aliased_default` is
; legitimate at `? y`, and the same rule referenced mandatorily at `x` must not make the spec
; unusable. Both use sites appear here so the split is pinned in one place.
mandatory_default = { a: uint, b: uint .default 3, ? c: uint .default 4 }

aliased_default = uint .default 9
alias_default_positions = { x: aliased_default, ? y: aliased_default }
